<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Paper 1 (calibration), the classical FMM baseline reported in Section V.

  This is the comparison arm, not a contribution of this paper: it is stock FMM with
  a fixed isotropic GPS error, scored with its own native Viterbi score. It is
  committed here because review item R8 asked for the configuration behind each table
  to be in the repository rather than reconstructed from memory, and because the
  numbers this file produces are what the manuscript calls "FMM native".

  THE SEARCH DISK IS THE SAME 300 m AS CaMM's, on purpose. r and gps_error are in
  network units, which on an EPSG:4326 network means degrees: 300 m = 300/111320 =
  0.0026949335 deg and 100 m = 100/111320 = 0.0008983112 deg. A source default of
  "300" would be 300 DEGREES; there is no usable default and the conversion is
  mandatory. This is the second time the project has had to write that sentence --
  an earlier revision ran the baseline at 0.03 deg, a 3.3 km disk, and reported the
  result as a fair comparison.

  tw_use_viterbi = true gives delta_t / sum_j delta_t(j). The filtering posterior
  alpha_t / sum_j alpha_t(j) is the SAME estimand CaMM reports and is the more
  informative comparison; it is obtained by setting this key to false, and it is
  reported in Table \ref{tab:audit} as S_2. The manuscript's main table uses the
  native score because that is what FMM actually reports.

  trustworthiness_smoothed is the alpha*beta smoother of the baseline's own
  forward recursion -- the FMM counterpart of the arm CaMM reports as its
  contribution. It is a second column off the same run, so the candidate stays
  fixed on the Viterbi path and the smoothed and filtering arms differ only in
  the evidence set. The backward pass is FastMapMatch::smooth_tg, and it always
  uses the forward probability alpha, whatever tw_use_viterbi says: at
  tw_use_viterbi = true the two columns are different estimands (delta_t /
  sum delta_t beside gamma_t), which is why they differ even on the last epoch
  of a trajectory, where beta == 1 and the smoothed score reduces to
  alpha_t / sum alpha_t.

  reverse_tolerance = 0.1 is a ratio of edge length, not a distance. The simulation
  corpus uses gps_error = 0.001 (111 m) instead of 0.0008983112 (100 m), because
  that is the value experiments/scripts/exp3_full_matching.py generated the corpus
  with; do not "fix" one to match the other without regenerating.

  Reproduce with:
      python3 experiments/scripts/real_final_tables.py
-->
<config>
  <input>
    <ubodt>
      <file>input/map/hainan_ubodt_indexed.bin</file>
    </ubodt>
    <network>
      <file>input/map/hainan/edges.shp</file>
      <id>key</id>
      <source>u</source>
      <target>v</target>
    </network>
    <gps>
      <file>data/real_vehicle/hainan_06/cmm_input_points_k1.csv</file>
      <gps_point/>
      <id>id</id>
      <x>x</x>
      <y>y</y>
      <timestamp>timestamp</timestamp>
    </gps>
  </input>
  <parameters>
    <k>16</k>
    <r>0.0026949335</r>
    <gps_error>0.0008983112</gps_error>
    <reverse_tolerance>0.1</reverse_tolerance>
    <tw_use_viterbi>true</tw_use_viterbi>
  </parameters>
  <output>
    <fields>
      <cpath/>
      <pgeom/>
      <trustworthiness/>
      <trustworthiness_smoothed/>
      <timestamp/>
      <candidates/>
    </fields>
    <file>data/archive_20260928/final/fmm.csv</file>
  </output>
  <other>
    <use_omp/>
    <input_epsg>4326</input_epsg>
  </other>
</config>
